#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Root helper for installable modules (installed as /usr/local/sbin/lab-module-helper; docs/catalog.md).

Socket-activated like the update helper (lab-module-helper.socket, Accept=yes): one process per request, ONE JSON line
in on stdin, ONE JSON line out. Only group lab (the gateway) and root (lab-admin) can connect. The gateway never writes
module code: it asks this helper, which accepts only these commands:

  {"op": "catalog-check", "index": URL}          fetch a module catalog (URL + URL.sig), verify its signature with the
                                                 trusted publisher keys (/etc/lab/publishers.d/*.pub, root-only) and
                                                 keep the verified copy → its modules, each version marked installable
                                                 here or not (min_core, svelte ABI, installed, bundled) and why
  {"op": "stage", "id": ID, "version": V        download V of ID from a catalog checked before (catalog: its URL when
   [, "catalog": URL]}                           several list it) into the helper's own state and verify it: sha256 from
                                                 the signed catalog, the package's own signature (the catalog's
                                                 publisher), no links or paths outside it, every file against its
                                                 MANIFEST, package.json + manifest (id, version, min_core, the svelte ABI,
                                                 connector port) → what the module asks for (the install dialog)
  {"op": "install", "id": ID, "version": V}      verify the staged V again and put it in /var/lib/lab-modules/ID/V
                                                 (root-owned, read-only for everyone else); ID must not be installed
  {"op": "update", "id": ID, "version": V}       the same for a newer V of an installed ID from the SAME publisher; the
                                                 version before stays on disk for a rollback (a running connector is
                                                 restarted on the new code)
  {"op": "rollback", "id": ID}                   back to the version before (verified again)
  {"op": "remove", "id": ID, "keep_config": B}   remove ID's files (its connector is stopped first). keep_config false
                                                 also removes its connector's key, unix user and state — unless a
                                                 bundled module of the same id still uses them (the gateway drops the
                                                 module's config from its database itself)
  {"op": "status"}                               installed modules, trusted publishers, checked catalogs, staged packages
  every request may carry "by": the user who asked (for the log)

Never followed or written: anything the gateway's user (or a connector's user) could create or replace (security review
2026-11-15, H2). Downloads go to this helper's own directory (root 0700); installed trees are created by root under
/var/lib/lab-modules (root 0755); the only thing taken from the gateway is the request line itself.

Refused: catalogs and packages not signed by a trusted publisher (a package must be signed by its catalog's publisher;
an update by the installed version's publisher), sha256 / MANIFEST mismatches, links or paths outside the package, a
package that replaces a bundled module unless it is newer and signed with the core's release key, min_core newer than
the core, a UI built for another svelte (major.minor) or importing what the shell does not export, a PRIVILEGED module
(`delegate: true` or `connector.uses_credentials_of`) from a publisher other than the core's release key unless root
allowed that publisher for it (/etc/lab/publishers.d/<name>.privileged: `lab-admin publisher allow-privileged NAME`),
a connector port another module uses, and URLs that are not https:// (plain http:// on the LAN only with LAB_UPDATE_ALLOW_HTTP=1 in
/etc/lab/update.env — the same test switch as core updates).

Command line (root):
  lab-module-helper verify-package FILE.tar.gz [PUBKEY]   check a package (signature with PUBKEY or the trusted
                                                          publishers, MANIFEST, package.json, manifest): the build uses it
  lab-module-helper publishers                            the trusted publisher keys"""
